    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d89">

                <pb facs="0196" n="114" xml:id="pg114"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">890d.00/232: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Beirut</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Knabenshue</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Beirut</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1925-10-29T12:00:00+02:00"><hi rendition="#italic">October 29,
                                1925—12 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received October 29—11:15
                        p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>I have received from High Commissioner written assurance requested by note quoted
                    in my telegram October 28, 4 p.m. He expresses strong conviction that uprising
                    here improbable. I concur but believe it is not impossible. Keeley reports by
                    telephone: (1) Only 2,200 rifles delivered to date; (2) destruction of houses
                    throughout the disturbed area has made many thousands homeless, many of whom are
                    joining ranks of brigands, numbers of whom [omission?] and now becoming
                    formidable; (3) French have apparently taken no precautionary measures to
                    prevent reentry of brigands into Damascus; (4) several districts near Damascus
                    in the hands of brigands have declared their independence.</p>
                <p>Brigand movement in Damascus and other regions shows indications of development
                    into a widespread organized revolutionary movement.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Knabenshue</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
